## Step 3: Writing the Actual Code

**In the `index.html` file:**

### The Seed Generator (Key Stream)

```javascript
var s = (key * 9973) % 2147483647;
```

This creates a starting seed from your key. Multiplying by `9973` (a prime number) spreads the seed across a large range so similar keys produce very different results.

---

### The XOR Pixel Scrambler

```javascript
function xorPixels(data) {
  var key = parseInt(document.getElementById('key').value) || 42;
  var out = new Uint8ClampedArray(data), s = (key * 9973) % 2147483647;
  for (var i = 0; i < out.length; i += 4) {
    s = (s * 16807) % 2147483647;
    var pk = Math.floor((s - 1) / 2147483646 * 256), px = i / 4;
    out[i]   ^= (key ^ pk ^ (px % 251))              & 0xFF;
    out[i+1] ^= ((key >> 3) ^ pk ^ ((px * 3) % 251)) & 0xFF;
    out[i+2] ^= ((key >> 5) ^ pk ^ ((px * 7) % 251)) & 0xFF;
  }
  return out;
}
```

This goes through every pixel (`i += 4` because each pixel has R, G, B, Alpha), generates a new pseudo-random value `pk`, and XORs each color channel with a unique combination of the key and `pk`.

---

### The Encrypt / Decrypt Toggle

```javascript
function run() {
  if (!isEncrypted) {
    encrypted = new ImageData(xorPixels(original.data), original.width, original.height);
    bx.putImageData(encrypted, 0, 0);
    isEncrypted = true;
  } else {
    bx.putImageData(new ImageData(xorPixels(encrypted.data), ...), 0, 0);
    isEncrypted = false;
  }
}
```

The same `xorPixels()` function is used for both encryption and decryption. When encrypting, it scrambles `original.data`. When decrypting, it unscrambles `encrypted.data` — same function, same key, opposite effect.

